  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 210 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nMonday through Saturday:\n\nHigh pressure gradually sinks builds across the Lower\nMississippi Valley on Monday, turning winds southwesterly and \nbringing a return to highs around 60 degrees. A strong 500 mb \nshortwave moving through central Ontario will allow for winds to\nremain gusty into the evening hours before diminishing. A weak \ncold front will follow, bringing northwest winds on Tuesday. \nStill anticipating temperatures around 60 degrees Tuesday \nafternoon before reinforcing cold high pressure builds in \nTuesday evening.\n\nWednesday, another shortwave propagating through the mean flow\naloft looks to move southeastward through the Canadian\nPrairies/northern Plains and bring a few chances for rain showers\n(15-20%) as well as highs in the low 50s with plenty of cloud \ncover. With this less intense shortwave, model uncertainty with \nplacement remains. Euro is the outlier at this time, showing a \nphasing, deepening system across Illinois as the shortwave swings \nfarther south and interacts with warm, moist air from the Gulf, \nwhile GFS/Canadian indicate a weaker system blending into the \nlarger semi-stationary low over the Canadian Maritimes. Kept NBM \nPoPs for this run due to this uncertainty, but confidence in some \nkind of scattered showers is increasing.\n\nMoving into Thursday, models have converged on a period of north\nto northeast flow, bringing large fetch and lake effect showers from\nLake Michigan throughout the day through the overnight hours and \nperhaps as late as Friday night before winds shift north to \nnorthwest and bring an end to precipitation. With Lake Michigan \nwater temperatures still in the 50s and inland temperatures \nfalling into the mid to low 30s inland, a mix of rain and snow is \npossible. At this time, not anticipating a transition to pure \nsnow, but colder trends will be monitored. Duration of lake effect\nprecipitation and cold air potential will be dependent on the \ndepth and placement of a developing Nor'easter along the Atlantic \ncoastal states, with a stronger, farther north solution being the \nbest for longer duration fetch through Friday night. Outside of \nlake effect regions, expect dry and cloudy conditions Thursday and\nFriday with highs in the mid-40s and lows around 30 degrees.\n\nHigh pressure returns for Saturday, with northwest winds and\nplenty of sunshine. Expect highs around 50 degrees.\n\nMH",
